      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#2 · the outline's thresholds and pathologies; the taste/knowing word-field as research route",
          "title": "Taste and chewing",
          "italic": "The mouth demonstrates discrimination maintaining contact with what it distinguishes — the relation does not wait for an etymology to license it, nor verify one in return.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The first bite has a taste: attraction, aversion and neutrality sit at the threshold before the material is worked through — a familiar account welcome, a contrary one provoking rejection. This first valuation belongs to the encounter and needs to become available to inquiry: liking an account and establishing its claim do different work. Chewing prolongs contact: teeth divide food so it can be received more fully; the epistemic act follows the texture of a claim — distinguishing a statement from its evidence, an event from its interpretation, a connection from the explanation offered for it; returning to the passage instead of letting the headline decide",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
